436
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:14,320
Let's break down the cosmological taxonomy presented here.

437
00:20:14,420 --> 00:20:20,900
The grand historical mystery that cosmologists have wrestled with for decades is that when we look at the very early universe,

438
00:20:21,280 --> 00:20:27,680
specifically the cosmic microwave background, or CMB, which is the oldest light we can see, it is shockingly smooth.

439
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:28,140
Yes.

440
00:20:28,480 --> 00:20:32,960
The temperature is basically exactly the same everywhere we look, down to tiny fractions of a degree.

441
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:36,380
This is known as CMB isotropy and large-scale homogeneity.

442
00:20:36,380 --> 00:20:36,820
Ah.

443
00:20:36,960 --> 00:20:40,520
The universe is, mathematically speaking, far too smooth.

444
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:40,760
Right.

445
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:55,360
Regions of space that are on opposite sides of the observable universe have the exact same temperature, even though they are so far apart that light, and therefore heat or information, should never have had time to travel between them to even things out.

446
00:20:55,360 --> 00:20:57,320
Which is the famous horizon problem.

447
00:20:57,380 --> 00:20:57,860
Exactly.

448
00:20:58,200 --> 00:21:03,080
So physicists had to invent mechanisms to explain this impossible smoothness.

449
00:21:03,380 --> 00:21:07,220
The framework breaks these historical attempts down into three classes.

450
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:08,600
Let's start with class one.

451
00:21:09,540 --> 00:21:10,340
Inflint and smoothing.

